winewayland: make an owned, managed window a SUBSURFACE of its owner

On Windows the app owns every window's position, so converting a point from one
window into another is exact. Wayland never tells a client where an
xdg_toplevel is, so Wine assumes the position Win32 asked for while the
compositor puts it somewhere else -- and any drag CROSSING two toplevels is off
by that constant difference.

Measured on Premiere 2026-08-25: dragging a button out of the Button Editor
onto the Program Monitor bar drops a constant few px to the right. Nothing else
in the app is affected, because no other drag crosses two toplevels. The user's
narrowing ("only that specific panel") is what made this findable.

Wine's input path is provably innocent -- with preserved-fixes/harnesses/
msgcoord.c the compositor-drawn cursor is correct, WM_MOUSEMOVE coordinates
convert back to GetCursorPos(), and button-DOWN and button-UP are exact. The
fault is the assumed ORIGIN of one window, not the coordinates.

A wl_subsurface is the one Wayland surface whose position the CLIENT sets
(wl_subsurface_set_position), so making an owned window a subsurface of its
owner restores the Windows model exactly: Wine states the position and that IS
the position. No inference, no compositor placement to discover, no drift.

The existing NEUTRON_DOCK_OWNED rule already embeds owned managed windows this
way but cannot fire here, for two reasons both visible in NEUTRON_POPUP_TRACE:

  * the Button Editor reports GW_OWNER = NULL, while the owner_hint win32u
    hands us is perfectly valid (owner_hint=0x10112 in every trace line);
  * it carries no WS_CAPTION -- style 0x96000000, a WS_POPUP tool window
    (WS_EX_NOACTIVATE|TOOLWINDOW|TOPMOST).

So: fall back to owner_hint when GW_OWNER is NULL, and do not require a
caption. Scope is owned + managed + visible owner; menus and other transients
are UNMANAGED and never reach this code, so they keep their popup path.

Gated NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE (default ON, =0 restores the old toplevel).

⚠️ Wider than one panel by design -- it reclassifies a whole window category,
so floating tool palettes across the suite take this path. Known pitfall from
the Lightroom filmstrip work: a re-shown subsurface comes back EMPTY, because
the initial-configure repaint a toplevel gets has no subsurface equivalent.
Hide/reshow is therefore a required test, not an optional one.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -301,6 +301,15 @@
     NtUserSetThreadDpiAwarenessContext(context);
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: see the OWNED-SUBSURFACE block in WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged. Default ON; set
+ * NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE=0 to fall back to an independent xdg_toplevel. */
+static BOOL neutron_owned_subsurface(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE"); on = (e && e[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
+    return on;
+}
+
 static BOOL wayland_win_data_create_wayland_surface(struct wayland_win_data *data,
                                                     struct wayland_surface *toplevel_surface,
                                                     struct wayland_surface *owner_surface,
@@ -744,6 +753,46 @@
                 toplevel = gwowner;
             }
         }
+
+        /* NEUTRON OWNED-SUBSURFACE (NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE=0 to disable).
+         *
+         * WHY. On Windows the APP owns every window's position, so converting a point from one
+         * window to another is exact. Wayland never tells a client where an xdg_toplevel is, so
+         * Wine has to assume the position Win32 asked for while the compositor puts it somewhere
+         * else -- and any drag CROSSING two toplevels is then off by that constant difference.
+         * Measured on Premiere 2026-08-25: dragging a button out of the Button Editor onto the
+         * Program Monitor bar drops a constant few px right, and nothing else in the app is
+         * affected because no other drag crosses two toplevels. The input path itself is provably
+         * clean (cursor, WM_MOUSEMOVE, button-down and button-up all exact).
+         *
+         * A wl_subsurface is the one Wayland surface whose position the CLIENT sets --
+         * wl_subsurface_set_position() -- so making an owned window a subsurface of its owner
+         * restores the Windows model exactly: Wine states the position, and that IS the position.
+         * No inference, no compositor placement to discover, no drift.
+         *
+         * Why the dock_owned rule above cannot do it: the Button Editor reports
+         * GW_OWNER = NULL (while the owner_hint handed to us is perfectly valid), and it carries
+         * NO WS_CAPTION -- style 0x96000000, a WS_POPUP tool window
+         * (WS_EX_NOACTIVATE|TOOLWINDOW|TOPMOST). Both of that rule's tests miss it.
+         *
+         * Scope: owned + managed + visible owner. Menus and other transients are unmanaged and
+         * never reach here, so they keep their popup path. */
+        if (neutron_owned_subsurface() && managed && !owner && toplevel == hwnd)
+        {
+            HWND gwowner = NtUserGetWindowRelative( hwnd, GW_OWNER );
+
+            /* GW_OWNER is NULL for exactly the windows this is meant to catch; owner_hint is the
+             * value win32u actually passed us and it is correct. */
+            if (!gwowner) gwowner = owner_hint;
+            if (gwowner && gwowner != hwnd && NtUserIsWindowVisible( gwowner ))
+            {
+                ERR( "neutron-ownedsub: hwnd=%p (style=%08x ex=%08x) -> SUBSURFACE of owner=%p "
+                     "so its position is ours to set, not the compositor's\n",
+                     hwnd, (unsigned)NtUserGetWindowLongW( hwnd, GWL_STYLE ),
+                     (unsigned)NtUserGetWindowLongW( hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE ), gwowner );
+                toplevel = gwowner;
+            }
+        }
     }
 
     /* GPU windows (splash screens, GL/Vulkan content) carry a client surface;
